# Example path-based gateway config — routes multiple apps under one Caddy instance
# instead of exposing each on its own port. Copy this file, replace the placeholder paths
# and ports below with your own, and keep your real, filled-in version out of version
# control (or in a private repo) since it will name your actual internal services.
#
# Every upstream here is reached via `host.docker.internal` (see docker-compose.yml's
# `extra_hosts`) — i.e. whatever port each app already publishes on this host. This
# gateway only adds a routing layer in front of already-running services; it doesn't
# replace or change any app's own deploy.
#
# IMPORTANT — basePath: an app mounted under a path prefix (e.g. /app1/*) needs to know
# about that prefix itself, or its HTML will load but request its own JS/CSS bundles from
# the wrong (root) path and 404. Framework-specific examples: Next.js's `basePath`/
# `assetPrefix` config (baked in at build time — a path-mounted build is a separate image
# from the app's normal root-mounted one, not a runtime toggle), or for a client-side-routed
# SPA, your bundler's `base` option plus your router's equivalent `basename`.

:80 {
	handle /app1* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3001
	}

	handle /app2* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3002
	}

	# The root/flagship app — no basePath needed, it already serves at "/".
	handle {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3000
	}
}
